@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# 回归报告 / Regression Report: local VoxCPM2 voice and joint tone
+
+## Change and necessity
+
+The user selected the tested local VoxCPM2 clone as primary speech. A disabled-by-default, exact-voice configuration now prefers a loopback HTTP worker for the selected online free voice. The original voice identity and online credentials stay in the existing resolver, providing native online failover when the local provider is excluded. Other voices, explicit providers and TTS disablement retain their routes.
+
+Before this change, the tested VoxCPM2 service could only be called by the isolated benchmark; live Neko used online speech. The new route sends reference-only streaming requests with native 48000 Hz PCM. It reuses the existing sentence ordering, cancellation, progress receipts and safe fallback suffix. It never forwards online credentials or voice IDs to the local endpoint and does not change general Custom API settings.
+
+## Tone without another generation
+
+The existing joint reply header accepts an optional bounded description, for example `[neko_emotion:happy|轻快、带笑意，语速适中]`. Old headers remain valid. The same main-brain generation supplies reply, expression and tone. The parser removes metadata before tool-leak guards, history and spoken text.
+
+Tone is captured by speech ID before buffering. Only the VoxCPM2 worker receives private style controls; only its HTTP payload adds the documented `(description)` prefix. Native sentence receipts, replay text, subtitles and online failover remain clean. A missing or invalid description uses a modest emotion/default tone without another model call. State is bounded and interrupted workers clear their style map.
+
+## Potential regressions and evidence
+
+Critical boundaries are exact-voice opt-in, local credential isolation, repeated provider selection after failure, sentence acknowledgements after styling, readiness buffers, overlapping speech IDs and interruption ownership. Tests cover these through the real HTTP sentence worker and the real native failover selector, with fake transport only where deterministic failures are required.
+
+Validation on 2026-10-11:
+
+- The routing, HTTP worker, joint protocol, cancellation, runtime handoff and Phase 2 selection passed 336 cases; the focused normalization/audio-cache/speech selection passed 88 cases. Counts overlap and are not added together.
+- Ruff passed for changed Python files. A live isolated worker emitted real 48 kHz PCM with happy and soft descriptions, acknowledged clean original sentences, emitted zero stale audio for six seconds after interruption, and synthesized the next turn successfully.
+- CPU ASR read back all three public test sentences without speaking the description. It showed small word/name variations; this is intelligibility evidence, not a subjective voice-similarity or emotion-quality score.
+- Broader watch-together coverage has one existing log-capture assertion failure: `test_failed_takeover_with_stale_token_still_unmutes`. The original committed host modules reproduce it (89 pass / 1 fail); stdout contains `takeover force-released`, and the ownership-release assertions pass. This unrelated logging/test issue was not changed. The focused selection above excludes that file.
+
+Actual persistent-service deployment, observed natural speech and full companion-bundle replay are recorded separately in the contribution fork. Cold startup and graphics-load latency must not be represented as warm steady-state or guaranteed playback latency.
